<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Second Turkic Khaganate</h1><p class="st-meta">682 CE – 744 CE · capital Otuken</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The revived Turkic empire of Mongolia, whose Orkhon inscriptions are the earliest surviving texts in a Turkic language.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After fifty years of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> rule, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilterish-qaghan">Ilterish Qaghan</a> led a revolt in 682 and restored an independent Turkic state, with the support of the strategist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonyukuk">Tonyukuk</a>. His brother Qapaghan extended its power from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagans</a> ruled from the Otuken highlands in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, leading a confederation of Turkic tribes in wars against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yenisei-kyrgyz">Kyrgyz</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/turgesh-khaganate">Turgesh Khaganate</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilge-qaghan">Bilge Qaghan</a> and his brother, the general Kul Tegin, the state reached its cultural height. Their memorial stelae on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkhon-river">Orkhon River</a>, written in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-turkic">Old Turkic</a> runic script, proclaim the duties of the khagan and warn the Turks against Chinese luxury.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilge-qaghan">Bilge Qaghan</a> was poisoned in 734, succession struggles weakened the khaganate. In 744 an alliance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basmyls">Basmyls</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/karluk-yabgu">Karluks</a> killed the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a>, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/uyghur-khaganate">Uyghur Khaganate</a> took its place.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="khanate, 682 CE – 744 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M18 74 V58 Q50 30 82 58 V74 Z"/><path d="M42 74 V60 H58 V74"/><path d="M30 50 L50 36 L70 50"/><path d="M50 36 V26"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">khanate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central Asia and the Steppe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:73.27%;width:1.23%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>682 CE – 744 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Otuken</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ilterish Qaghan, Qapaghan Qaghan, Bilge Qaghan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tengrism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Turkic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, <a href="/countries/china">China</a>, <a href="/countries/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Turk Bilge Qaghanate, Orkhon Turks</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=713&amp;focus=second-turkic-khaganate" aria-label="Second Turkic Khaganate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7280 558 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Second Turkic Khaganate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7600 814L7600 814M7600 814L7400 770M7600 814L6820 913"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7600 814h0M7400 770h0M6820 913h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7600 814h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7600 814h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=713&amp;focus=second-turkic-khaganate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
